'''2.''' Rousing from sleep; exciting into motion or action.—'''3.''' Passed in the waking state; experienced while awake: as, ''waking'' hours.

{{smaller block|Such sober certainty of ''waking'' bliss.
{{right|''Milton'', Comus, l. 263.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Waking numbness''', a numbness and tingling lasting for a short time, sometimes experienced upon first waking from sleep, but soon disappearing.}}

'''waking''' (wā′king), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wakinge, wakynge, wacunge''; verbal n. of ''wake''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1.''' The act of passing from sleep to wakefulness, or of causing another so to pass.

{{smaller block|They sleep secure from ''waking.''
{{right|''Cowper'', Friendship, l. 123.}}}}

'''2.''' The state or period of being awake.

{{smaller block|His sleeps and his ''wakings'' are so much the same that he knows not how to distinguish them.
{{right|''S. Butler'', Characters.}}}}

'''3†.''' Watch.

{{smaller block|Aboute the fourth ''waking'' of the night.
{{right|''Wyclif'', Mark vi. 48.}}}}

'''4.''' A vigil; especially, the act of holding a wake, or of watching the dead.

{{smaller block|To speken of bodily peyne, it stant in preyeres, in ''wakynges'', in fastynges, in vertuouse techinges of orisouns.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Parson’s Tale.}}}}

